==== The FMC, Step by Step

The Flight Management Computer is the 737's cockpit brain: route, performance, and vertical profile in one box. Learn the flow once, and every FMC in this guide — the Q400's, the 777's — is the same idea in different clothes: a set of pages you work through in a fixed order, entering data and cross-checking what the box thinks it knows. Exact page names follow the aircraft's manual; the order is universal, and the order is the skill.

===== The Pages

An FMC is a filing cabinet of pages, and the work flow is the same every flight. You begin with the identity and initial pages — who the aircraft is and where it is. Then the route: the flight plan, waypoint by waypoint, with the departure and arrival procedures added from the DEP/ARR pages. Then the performance pages, where the numbers live: weights, fuel, speeds, and the takeoff data the box computes from the runway and the weather. Then the LEGS page, the route in detail — each leg, its course, its distance, and the vertical constraints — which is the page you check twice, because it is where the box shows you exactly what it thinks it is going to fly.

===== Load the Plan

The plan lives first outside the box, in LittleNavMap, built and checked while the aircraft is parked: route, altitudes, the SID and STAR you expect, and the approach. Enter it into the FMC — waypoint by waypoint, or from a saved plan where the aircraft supports it — and watch the box build the legs as you go; the box should confirm the plan, never invent it. Add the procedures: DEP for the departure, ARR for the arrival and the approach, and the FMC inserts the published fixes and the vertical profile. Then cross-check the LEGS page against the briefing, leg by leg: distances sane, altitudes legal, nothing doubled. It is the step that catches the mistakes, and it is the step the hurried pilot skips.

===== Performance & Takeoff

With the route in, the performance pages turn the day into numbers: the zero fuel weight, the fuel load, and the V-speeds the box computes — V1, Vr, V2 — for the runway and the weather you entered. Arm the thrust and the auto-throttle for the departure, and brief the takeoff out loud: the speeds, the departure, the abort plan. The box is now carrying everything it needs; the takeoff itself is the part you fly by hand and by the numbers, with the box as the source of those numbers.

===== Fly the Path

After takeoff, the box starts earning its keep. LNAV — lateral navigation — flies the route turn by turn: engage it when the departure allows, and watch the first turn, because the first turn tells you whether the box has the plan you think it has. VNAV — vertical navigation — flies the profile: the climb at the computed speeds, the level-offs at the constraints, and the descent beginning at the computed top of descent. The box only knows what you entered, so the constraints on the LEGS page are the contract, and the descent is the moment of truth: if it looks early or late, the energy is the lever — speedbrakes and speed, not panic — and a well-programmed box makes the arrival a quiet confirmation rather than a rescue.

===== The Arrival & Approach

The arrival and approach are the box's last act, and yours is the briefing. As the descent begins, load the arrival and arm the approach: the ILS frequency or the RNAV approach, the minimums set, the final approach course in the box. Watch the captures — the localizer, then the glideslope — and let the box fly the approach while you fly the briefing: the decision altitude, the runway environment, and the call. The box does the flying; the pilot does the deciding, and the deciding is the job.

===== The Autoland Brief

Some 737 approaches can go all the way to touchdown on the automation, and an autoland deserves the same briefing as any approach: the setup, the path, the minimums, the missed — plus the autoland specifics, the dual autopilots engaged where the aircraft requires it, the flare armed. Then monitor like a hawk: the needles, the speed, and the mode annunciations, because the automation is a tool, not a witness. At minimums the decision is still yours: continue to the automatic flare, or take it by hand. The point of the autoland brief is not the novelty of hands-off landing; it is that even the most automated approach in the airplane belongs to the same discipline as the first one you briefed in Wave 2.

===== The Cross-Check Habit

The FMC is only as good as its data, and its data is only as good as your entry — so the habit is a rhythm of checks, each cheap and each load-bearing: once on the ground, the route you loaded matches the plan you briefed; once in the climb, the LEGS page still matches the clearance; once before the approach, the loaded approach is the briefed approach. The box gets no free pass, ever. That is the airline way, and it is the point of Wave 4: the automation is powerful, and the pilot who treats it as a proposal to be verified, every time, is the pilot the automation can never surprise.
